  message: "SECOND RESIDUAL MEASURED. This item and INV-lupav both carry it, filed and\nexplicitly never measured: \"cfg.uncovered_call_lines keys on CALL nodes only, so\na def/use omission at a NON-call construct is invisible to the gate. Read from\ncfg.py:1457; not measured.\" It is now measured, on two languages.\n\nRESULT -- the gate has a large, LANGUAGE-SPECIFIC hole:\n\n  substrate                    GATE BLIND   positive control   denominator\n  hypergumbo / python            31.9%          33.7%             1,398\n  alertmanager / go               3.1%          20.3%             1,497\n  prometheus  / go                7.8%          17.5%             5,014\n\nGATE BLIND = the function holds a definition the CFG recorded no statement for,\nAND uncovered_call_lines returns empty, so refutation is still permitted. That\nis precisely INV-lupav L2's shape (\"a partially-recorded definition still\nreturns False\") surviving the gate built to stop it.\n\nDOMINANT CONSTRUCTS, and they are DISJOINT between the two languages -- the same\nsplit the forfeit-share measurement found, arriving independently:\n  python   for_statement 544, for_in_clause 385   (loop + comprehension binding)\n  go       short_var_declaration 61 / 821, assignment_statement, var_declaration,\n           inc_statement, dec_statement\n\nDENOMINATOR is functions PRODUCING def/use -- the only population section 3a can\nact on. Functions with no defines are reported separately and never folded in\n(python 534, alertmanager 1,083, prometheus 3,526). It INCLUDES test files;\nscoping to production was not applied and would change the numbers.\n\nINSTRUMENT, entirely production's own: the registered LanguageDdgSpec supplies\nthe file glob and function node types, plus load_cfg_mapping,\nbuild_function_cfg, populate_def_use_for_cfg, the registered def/use extractor,\nand uncovered_call_lines itself. No hand-rolled \"which constructs bind a name\"\npredicate -- that is the extractor's job and re-deriving it is the shape this\nrepo keeps getting wrong.\n\nTWO INSTRUMENT FAULTS FOUND AND FIXED BEFORE ANY NUMBER WAS BELIEVED, recorded\nbecause the first one nearly became a reported null:\n\n 1. THE FIRST RUN PRINTED \"NO FUNCTIONS PRODUCED def/use\" -- a clean-looking\n    zero. It was not a finding: build_function_cfg takes a symbol_id I was not\n    passing, the TypeError was being swallowed by a broad except into a bucket\n    the zero-denominator branch then exited without printing. That is the trap\n    INV-lupav's own description warns about in these words -- \"a reconstruction\n    that omits callees_at/summaries returns None in every arm INCLUDING the\n    positive control, which means the probe never reached the walk... that trap\n    has now caught two separate attempts; do not make it a third.\" It very\n    nearly did. The instrument now prints the explaining buckets and exits 1\n    rather than 0 on an empty denominator, so a fault cannot read as a result.\n\n 2. THE FIRST WORKING VERSION ASKED THE WRONG QUESTION. It tested BYTE\n    CONTAINMENT -- \"is this node inside some recorded statement's extent\" --\n    which a for_statement can never satisfy, because it SPANS its own body and\n    the recorded statements are inside it. Every loop looked uncovered. The\n    question that matters to the walk is whether the DDG holds a definition for\n    the NAME at all, so the test is now name coverage. The number went UP\n    (25.8% -> 31.9%), not down: containment was over-reporting loops and\n    under-reporting comprehensions (for_in_clause appeared only after the fix,\n    and the assignment / augmented_assignment entries vanished because those\n    names WERE defined by some other recorded statement).\n\nDIRECT DEMONSTRATION, an 8-line function, so the mechanism is not inferred from\naggregates:\n\n    def handler(items, flag):\n        total = 0\n        for item in items:          <- binds `item`\n            total += item           <- USES `item`\n        with open(\"f\") as fh:       <- binds `fh`\n            data = fh.read()        <- USES `fh`\n        return total, data\n\n  CFG records 6 statements; the names it holds a definition for are exactly\n  {total, data}. `item` and `fh` are held by nothing. Neither construct\n  necessarily contains a call, so uncovered_call_lines is empty and refutation\n  is permitted for values the walk provably cannot follow.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT IS A LOWER BOUND, and this is the part not to lose: `fh` does\nnot appear in the count at all. The extractor does not report the with-as target\nas a definition either, so a function whose ONLY unrecorded binding is a\ncontext-manager target is scored CLEAN here. The instrument can only see\ndefinitions the extractor names.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CHANGE.\n\n  It does NOT make wiring the section 3a arm actionable. That arm still tests\n  `is True` (verified in code today at taint.py:2953, against the barrier arm's\n  `is False` at taint.py:3009), so False and None still collapse there and the\n  wiring is still a no-op that must land with WI-kabif's removal authority --\n  which is out of scope by owner ruling. Nothing here disturbs that sequencing.\n\n  It DOES price the gate honestly for the moment removal authority is granted.\n  The forfeit share (12.8-35.5%) was read as \"the gate WOULD do work\". This says\n  how much work it would MISS: on python, roughly a third of walkable functions\n  would be permitted to refute while holding a definition the DDG never\n  recorded. A gate that covers the call-shaped gaps and not the binding-shaped\n  ones is not the safety property either item's statement claims.\n\n  It is ALSO the answer to \"is the gate's call-only keying good enough\" -- no,\n  and the deficit is where the languages differ, which a per-language gap list\n  would have got wrong in exactly the way coverage-gating was chosen to avoid.\n\nSUGGESTED SHAPE, not costed: extend uncovered_call_lines (or add a sibling with\nthe same forfeit consumer) to key on NODES THE EXTRACTOR REPORTS AS DEFINING\nwhose names no recorded statement carries -- language-agnostic, because the\nextractor is already the per-language authority, and it needs no new\nper-language list. The with-as blindness is upstream of that and is a def/use\nextractor gap in its own right."  # cfd3
